* Files kept from the interrupted attempt already existed, but the word oracle failed with 12 differences. They were all noise: the sdkjs changes (font check on remote changes, style cache, shape cache) don't change results. On top of that, some \"same\" results meant nothing, because the scripted edit had never reached the document.\n\n**Decisions:**\n- **Edits:** text and bold now go through the editor's API instead of simulated keystrokes, and every step waits until co-editing has the server's lock answer. Without that wait, input sent in the meantime is dropped without any error, which is why words went missing.\n- **Comparing what each user sends:** messages are combined into one stream, because how changes are grouped into messages depends on timing. The random paragraph ids in new objects are masked out. Seeding the random generator alone wasn't enough.\n- **Comparing what's shown:** the screen hash is taken only once two reads in a row agree, with the cursor hidden.\n- **Slides:** B switches page and selects the shape in separate steps. Both users leave text editing, and B leaves before A saves, because B's held lock blocked A's download.\n- **New check:** each side's document must contain the scripted edits, or the case fails. It caught two false passes, both now fixed. In `chart.pptx`, A had been selecting the chart rather than a text shape. In both slide cases, B's edit had never landed.\n- **Builds:** `build.sh` builds the two bundle variants one at a time with a capped heap. If a minified build is still killed for lack of memory, it rebuilds without minifying and the script output says so. In the final runs everything was minified.\n- **Sensitivity:** a copy of the new bundle that turns typed `n` into `N` was flagged in five checks (document, A's changes, screen, reopened file, converted file) and the run failed. B's changes stayed the same, which is correct since B types no `n`.\n\n**Problems and gaps:**\n- The shell's memory limit (6.27 GB) is shared with the other agents. The slide build was killed five times while another packet compiled x2t, and I had to wait for that compile to finish.\n- The timing lines show no measurable gain on these small inputs: about 4.3 s to open and 3 s to edit on both sides, which is within run-to-run variation.\n- No case makes a remote change to a chart itself; only text is edited.\n- No intended differences are listed yet.\n- No permission was refused.\n\n**Lessons for the Atlas** (written in the packet):\n- In co-editing, wait for the lock answer after every action, or input is lost silently.\n- Paragraph ids are random, so any two runs differ in those bytes.\n- The memory limit is shared between agents.\n- A differential oracle needs a check that its edits landed, or \"same\" can pass with nothing edited.\n\nThe friction line is appended to `.theseus/scoreboard/friction.jsonl`.","ttft_ms":4248,"type":"result","duration_ms":3763288,"uuid":"c0cd59ed-f824-439d-8b93-775f30525c03","ttft_stream_ms":3376,"time_to_request_ms":342,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":6,"system_prompt":223,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":11,"input_attachments":20,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":8,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":8,"init_frame_send":3,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":9,"query_setup":4,"auto_mode_context_wait":7,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":21,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":6},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":52,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":147,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":208,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":137,"process_rss_mib_result":216,"process_anon_mib_result":148,"process_peak_rss_mib":241,"process_cpu_ms_turn":30477,"process_wall_ms_turn":3763287,"process_cpu_ms_streaming":2997,"process_wall_ms_streaming":195878,"main_stream_count":47,"main_stream_ev